From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 03:46:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11187/11362] Memory index drift: quoting-normalized
 descriptions, fail-open hook, ops record

Found live on the first real append: the Edit tool's memory tooling
re-serializes frontmatter on write (the description gains YAML quotes
with escaped inner quotes; metadata.modified is stamped). Hashed raw,
that read as the author moving the description, which (a) silenced the
point-of-append reminder on exactly the append that caused it and (b)
let the dashboard clear a flag when only the index line moved.
Description hashes now use the unquoted text (normalize_description).
The first test for this let the mutation SURVIVE, because its baseline
was unquoted and hash(normalize(quoted)) == hash(raw unquoted); it was
rewritten to the case where normalization changes the outcome, and both
hash sites are now proven by mutation (M10 snapshot, M11 hook).

The hook entrypoint now fails OPEN: any exception exits 0 and prints
nothing. A reminder must never break an edit; the dashboard is the
fail-closed layer.

Also: ruff format on both files (the conductor checkpoint fb92b677ce that
swept the code runs no hooks); ops/changelog, ops/status, traceability.

Mutation gate: the pending marker from the pt4 test run named one path,
ops/artifact_convention_audit_report.md, marked [NOT this run] -- a
conductor audit report regenerated during the pytest window, not test
damage. Deleted deliberately per the gate's own instruction; no path was
reverted.

